WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:04.613
Odysseus was chopping wood for the cooking fires when Calypso came to him.

2
00:00:04.614 --> 00:00:10.425
"I have news," she said. Odysseus stopped chopping the wood and asked, "What news?"

3
00:00:10.426 --> 00:00:13.248
"It is time for you to leave," she replied.

4
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:18.120
"Zeus' errand boy has spoken with me, and you are to make a raft and leave my island."

5
00:00:18.121 --> 00:00:23.465
"Leave on a raft?" answered Odysseus in dismay. "That would be suicide."

6
00:00:23.466 --> 00:00:28.613
"Zeus has commanded, and you and I must obey," she smiled scornfully and continued.

7
00:00:28.614 --> 00:00:31.825
"Hermes has assured me that you will be safe enough."

8
00:00:31.826 --> 00:00:40.263
Calypso turned and walked away, and Odysseus looked into the sky and asked, "Am I truly free? Give me a sign so that I may know it is true."

9
00:00:40.265 --> 00:00:44.232
Athena heard Odysseus and she sent her owl shooting through the sky.

10
00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:49.654
Odysseus saw the omen and knew that Athena had answered him, and joy burst from his heart.

11
00:00:49.656 --> 00:00:53.810
When Odysseus finished the raft, he and Calypso had a special meal.

12
00:00:53.811 --> 00:00:58.727
"I have grown fond of you," said Calypso. "I will miss you when you are gone."

13
00:00:58.728 --> 00:01:01.568
Odysseus thanked her and pushed his raft into the sea.

14
00:01:01.569 --> 00:01:07.090
For seventeen days Odysseus sailed uneventfully, but then Poseidon noticed him just as he sighted land.

15
00:01:07.091 --> 00:01:11.553
Poseidon, who was still angry with him, smashed his raft with a terrible storm.

16
00:01:11.554 --> 00:01:15.600
The raft shattered and Odysseus clung to a log while the tempest raged.

17
00:01:15.601 --> 00:01:20.528
The next morning Odysseus awoke and, as he was lying on the sand, he could hear voices in the distance.

18
00:01:20.529 --> 00:01:27.344
Odysseus carefully searched for the source of these voices and he found that several young maidens were doing their laundry in a stream.

19
00:01:27.345 --> 00:01:31.433
When Odysseus announced himself all the maidens ran away except for Nausicaa.

20
00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:36.935
"Are you a beggar or a thief?" she asked. "I am neither," replied Odysseus.

21
00:01:36.936 --> 00:01:41.015
"Are you a goddess or a nymph? For you are too beautiful to be mortal."

22
00:01:41.016 --> 00:01:47.843
The princess giggled and replied, "I am neither. I am Nausicaa, the daughter of Alcinous, the king."

23
00:01:47.844 --> 00:01:56.725
Odysseus bowed to the princess and said, "Beautiful maiden, take me to your father, for I have a sad story to tell, and a favor to ask."

24
00:01:56.726 --> 00:02:02.003
Nausicaa looked at Odysseus and said, "I will take you to my father, but first you must wash.

25
00:02:02.004 --> 00:02:04.098
I will wait over there with my maids."

26
00:02:04.099 --> 00:02:13.731
When Odysseus looked into the still pool, he was shocked by his gruesome features—his clothes were ripped, torn, and stained with blood, his face was bruised, swollen, and covered with grime.

27
00:02:13.732 --> 00:02:18.245
He looked like a lunatic, and he was amazed that Nausicaa hadn't run away.

28
00:02:18.246 --> 00:02:25.134
Nausicaa took Odysseus to her father, and after he heard Odysseus' story he gave Odysseus both gold and passage by ship.

29
00:02:25.135 --> 00:02:31.507
The ship took Odysseus back to Ithaca. When he arrived home he could barely recognize it because the island was covered in fog.

30
00:02:31.508 --> 00:02:35.642
As soon as he stepped off the ship, he dropped to his knees and kissed the earth.

31
00:02:35.643 --> 00:02:39.840
Tears filled his eyes as he remembered the twenty long years that he had been away.

32
00:02:39.841 --> 00:02:46.943
"Rise, Odysseus, and welcome home," said a voice out of the mist. Odysseus stood and found himself in front of Athena.

33
00:02:46.944 --> 00:02:49.827
"There is trouble in your house," she continued.

34
00:02:49.828 --> 00:02:56.546
"A large group of suitors have come to take your wife's hand. They dishonor you and your son. You must take revenge."

35
00:02:56.547 --> 00:03:00.879
"Great goddess, how should I strike them?" asked Odysseus.

36
00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:06.975
"You will know how, when the time is right, but first you must learn who has been loyal to you and who has been disloyal.

37
00:03:06.976 --> 00:03:14.110
I will conceal your identity until it is time for your revenge." But before Odysseus could reply, the goddess was gone.

38
00:03:14.111 --> 00:03:17.650
The first person that Odysseus met was the swineherd Eumaeus.

39
00:03:17.651 --> 00:03:25.618
Eumaeus told Odysseus about the trouble that the suitors had caused; eating all the food, drinking all the wine, and slaughtering the herds like there was a feast every night.

40
00:03:25.619 --> 00:03:29.869
"Why doesn't the master of the house stop this?" asked Odysseus.

41
00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.453
"It is because the gods have cursed him.

42
00:03:32.454 --> 00:03:39.087
They have kept him away from home for a long time, but his son is still loyal and so are many of the servants."

43
00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:45.524
Odysseus looked at the swineherd and said, "I believe you to be one of those loyal servants, and I bless you."

44
00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:50.826
While Odysseus was speaking with the swineherd, Athena found Telemachus, Odysseus' son.

45
00:03:50.827 --> 00:03:56.484
"Your father will soon return," she said. "Find the swineherd, and prepare for his arrival."

46
00:03:56.485 --> 00:04:03.040
Telemachus did not recognize the goddess, but he saw a falcon strike a dove, and he knew that the stranger had spoken the truth.

47
00:04:03.041 --> 00:04:07.667
Telemachus immediately went in search of the swineherd. He found the swineherd at home.

48
00:04:07.668 --> 00:04:11.333
"Loyal Eumaeus, I have news," called Telemachus.

49
00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:15.920
"Soon our lord and my father will return. Go and tell the others."

50
00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:19.493
"I knew he would come back," said the swineherd.

51
00:04:19.494 --> 00:04:23.643
"I'll wait for your return," said Telemachus, "so be quick."

52
00:04:23.644 --> 00:04:27.778
The swineherd rushed off at once. Inside the hut Athena had appeared.

53
00:04:27.779 --> 00:04:36.802
"Odysseus, it is time. Stand and be ready to greet your son." When Telemachus opened the door to the swineherd's hut, there was a blinding flash of light.

54
00:04:36.803 --> 00:04:40.514
"What god are you?" asked Telemachus when he saw his father.

55
00:04:40.515 --> 00:04:45.484
"I am no god," answered Odysseus. "I am your father."

56
00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:52.992
Telemachus at first doubted Odysseus' claim and then Athena said, "Do not doubt, noble Telemachus, for there is much work to do."

57
00:04:52.993 --> 00:04:59.825
"Yes, my son, it is time to plan our revenge, and I will need your help if we are to succeed," said Odysseus.

58
00:04:59.826 --> 00:05:03.190
Telemachus returned home and prepared for his father's revenge.

59
00:05:03.191 --> 00:05:07.928
First, he gathered up and hid all the weapons. He then told his mother to have a contest.

60
00:05:07.929 --> 00:05:13.031
The suitor who could string Odysseus' bow and shoot an arrow through twelve hoops could marry her.

61
00:05:13.032 --> 00:05:18.079
Athena again placed Odysseus in disguise, and he went with the swineherd to his home.

62
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.777
The suitors were feasting when Odysseus and the swineherd arrived.

63
00:05:21.778 --> 00:05:31.489
The suitors treated Odysseus the beggar and the swineherd badly, and in anger Odysseus said, "You who feast on another's food, are unwilling to spare a crust of bread for me.

64
00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:33.420
You are worse than thieves!"

65
00:05:33.421 --> 00:05:38.745
The suitors were angered by the beggar's harsh words, but before they could punish him, Penelope arrived.

66
00:05:38.746 --> 00:05:41.383
"I have made a decision," she said.

67
00:05:41.384 --> 00:05:49.642
"My lord and husband will never return, so I will marry the man who can string my husband's bow and shoot an arrow through twelve golden hoops."

68
00:05:49.643 --> 00:05:52.690
Each suitor approached the bow and each suitor failed to string it.

69
00:05:52.691 --> 00:05:57.118
"Is there no one among you as good as my husband?" called Penelope to the suitors.

70
00:05:57.119 --> 00:06:04.531
"Mother," interrupted Telemachus, "there may be one here among these men who can, but I doubt if the suitors will allow him to try.

71
00:06:04.532 --> 00:06:10.332
He is my guest, and so he shall try in my name, for a son cannot compete for his mother's hand."

72
00:06:10.333 --> 00:06:13.175
Telemachus snatched the bow and handed it to the beggar.

73
00:06:13.176 --> 00:06:15.253
The beggar took the bow and quickly strung it.

74
00:06:15.254 --> 00:06:21.095
A loud clap of thunder boomed in the distance as Odysseus fitted an arrow to the string and raised the bow.

75
00:06:21.096 --> 00:06:27.690
Seemingly without aiming, Odysseus let the arrow fly and it unerringly passed through each of the twelve hoops.

76
00:06:27.691 --> 00:06:31.412
A flash of lightning shot across the sky and the beggar was transformed.

77
00:06:31.413 --> 00:06:39.270
"I have returned!" shouted Odysseus as he fitted arrows to his bow and slew the suitors where they stood. "And this is my due."

78
00:06:39.271 --> 00:06:45.683
Telemachus and the loyal servant also joined the fight, and soon all the suitors and those disloyal to Odysseus were slain.

79
00:06:45.684 --> 00:06:50.683
"The battle is won," said Odysseus to his son, "but our home is befouled.

80
00:06:50.684 --> 00:06:54.734
Let the servants clean the house before we celebrate my homecoming."

81
00:06:54.735 --> 00:07:00.560
Once the remains from the battle had been cleaned away, Odysseus presented himself formally to his wife, Penelope.

82
00:07:00.561 --> 00:07:06.137
She was still uncertain about his identity, so she quizzed him about their bedroom and the bed that they had shared.

83
00:07:06.138 --> 00:07:13.936
Odysseus described how he had made it out of a single piece of cedar, and Penelope rejoiced because her husband had finally returned.

